        "## 14. Conclusions, Limitations \n",
        "\n",
        "### Results: \n",
        "Both models performed excellently on the held-out test set. The scratch CNN reached 100.0% test accuracy (macro-F1 1.000) with only 1.17M parameters, training for 21 of 25 epochs before early stopping, at ~76 ms mean inference latency and a 14.1 MB export size. The fine-tuned EfficientNetB0 reached 99.92% test accuracy (macro-F1 0.999) with 4.38M parameters across 20 epochs (10 frozen + 10 fine-tuned), ~83 ms mean latency, and a 33.0 MB export size. Given how close the two are on accuracy, the extra size and complexity of EfficientNetB0 isn't clearly worth it here, the scratch CNN is smaller, faster, and just as accurate, which suggests the ASL Alphabet dataset (plain background, one subject, consistent framing) is constrained enough that a simple, purpose-built CNN already solves it near-perfectly.\n",
        "\n",
        "Limitations \n",
        "1. J and Z are motion letters: they require a finger wiggle/trace; any static-frame\n",
        "   classifier (ours included) cannot represent the motion, only an approximate handshape.\n",
        "2. Studio-vs-webcam domain gap:  training photos are clean, centred and consistently\n",
        "   lit; live webcam frames are noisier, so expect some accuracy drop in the live demo.\n",
        "3. No hand ROI cropping:  full frames include sleeves/background, so some signal\n",
        "   leaks in from context rather than the hand itself.\n",
